broken `Dockerfile` can no longer surface only after the crate has been published (issue #808).\n- Pull-request job validating\"\"\")\ns=s.replace(\"\"\"### Fixed\"\"\",\"\"\"### Fixed\n- Replaced the contradictory `always() && !cancelled()` job guards in the CI/CD pipeline with `!cancelled()`, so cancelling a run actually stops the dependent jobs (issue #808).\"\"\")\nopen(p,'w').write(s)\nPY\ngit add -A && git commit -q -F - <<'EOF'\nci: gate the Docker image on pull requests and fix contradictory job guards\n\nIssue #808, second batch of pull-request gates.\n\nThe Dockerfile was only built inside auto-release / manual-release, i.e.\n*after* the crate was published to crates.io, so a broken image produced a\nhalf-finished release that could not be caught before merge (runs\n29312084458 and 29485000765). scripts/verify-docker-runtime.sh, the\nruntime contract the image installs as verify-formal-ai-dind, had no\ncaller anywhere in CI. The new docker-build job builds with\npush:false/load:true -- so it also works for fork pull requests, which\nhave no registry credentials -- behind a GHA layer cache, then runs the\nverifier in a privileged container so the inner daemon assertion is real.\n\nSeparately, nine jobs guarded on `always() && !cancelled()`. always()\nmakes a job run even when the run is cancelled, which is precisely what\nthe second half tries to forbid; per CI-CD-BEST-PRACTICES.md section 10\nthe correct guard is `!cancelled()` alone. One of the nine is an inline\n`if:` and had to be wrapped in ${{ }}, since a bare leading `!` is a YAML\ntag indicator.\n\nFormal-AI-Session: issue-808-claude-20260720\nFormal-AI-Evidence: dev/log/issues/808/pulls/809/analysis.md\nEOF\nrust-script scripts/self-hosting-metric.rs --since main --until HEAD 2>&1 | tail -2 && git push -q origin issue-808-bf5af32dbcdd && echo pushed"
