[2026-07-20T08:40:33.046Z] [INFO]           "command": "cat > /tmp/tpl/rust-issue.md <<'EOF'\nFound while auditing a downstream consumer of this template (`link-assistant/formal-ai`, issue [#808](https://github.com/link-assistant/formal-ai/issues/808)). Three of the defects we hit are present in this template itself, so downstream repos inherit them.\n\n## 1. `always() && !cancelled()` is self-contradictory (7 occurrences)\n\n```console\n$ gh api repos/link-foundation/rust-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/contents/.github/workflows/release.yml \\\n    -q .content | base64 -d | grep -c 'always() && !cancelled()'\n7\n```\n\n`always()` is documented to make a job/step run **even if the workflow run is cancelled**. `!cancelled()` is there to express the opposite. When both appear in one expression the `always()` wins for the cancellation case, so `!cancelled()` is dead weight that reads as if it were doing something — a maintenance trap: anyone reading the guard believes cancelling the run stops the job, and it does not.\n\n`link-assistant/hive-mind`'s `docs/CI-CD-BEST-PRACTICES.md` §10 prescribes `!cancelled()` alone, and this template's **js** sibling already does exactly that (0 occurrences of the combined form; see `js-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template/.github/workflows/release.yml:557`).\n\n**Fix:** replace `always() && !cancelled()` with `!cancelled()`. One caveat we hit downstream: a single-line `if: !cancelled() && …` is invalid YAML, because a leading `!` is a tag indicator. Either wrap it — `if: ${{ !cancelled() && … }}` — or use a block scalar `if: |`.\n\n## 2. The Dockerfile is never built at pull-request stage\n\nThe image is built and pushed only inside the release jobs, i.e. *after* `cargo publish` has already run. A Dockerfile regression therefore cannot fail a pull request; it produces a half-finished release instead — crate on crates.io, no image, no GitHub Release. We hit exactly that downstream (runs 29312084458 and 29485000765).\n\n**Fix (what we shipped downstream):** a pull-request job that builds without pushing, so it also works for fork PRs that have no registry credentials:\n\n```yaml\n  docker-build:\n    name: Docker Image Build Check\n    runs-on: ubuntu-latest\n    timeout-minutes: 60      # the image compiles the crate; 30 is not enough cold\n    needs: [detect-changes]\n    if: github.event_name == 'pull_request' && needs.detect-changes.outputs.any-code-changed == 'true'\n    steps:\n      - uses: actions/checkout@v6\n      - run: bash scripts/free-runner-disk.sh\n      - uses: docker/setup-buildx-action@v4\n      - uses: docker/build-push-action@v7\n        with:\n          context: .\n          push: false\n          load: true\n          tags: app:pr-check\n          cache-from: type=gha\n          cache-to: type=gha,mode=max\n```\n\n**Workaround** for repos that cannot adopt the job: run `docker build .` in an existing PR job, accepting that it does not share the buildx layer cache with the release build.\n\n## 3. No `simulate-fresh-merge.sh` and no secrets scan\n\nBoth exist in the js sibling and neither exists here:\n\n```console\n$ grep -n 'simulate-fresh-merge\\|secretlint' js-.../release.yml\n118:        run: bash scripts/simulate-fresh-merge.sh\n234:        run: npx --yes -p secretlint -p @secretlint/secretlint-rule-preset-recommend secretlint \"**/*\"\n$ grep -c 'simulate-fresh-merge\\|secretlint' rust-.../release.yml\n0\n```\n\nConsequence: a pull request that is green in isolation can still break `main` (semantic merge conflict), and a committed credential is never flagged. `secretlint` is language-agnostic and runs fine in a Rust repo via `npx`; `simulate-fresh-merge.sh` is pure shell.\n\nHappy to open pull requests for any of the three if that is useful.\nEOF\ngh issue create --repo link-foundation/rust-ai-driven-development-pipeline-template --title \"CI: contradictory always() && !cancelled() guards, no Docker PR gate, missing fresh-merge simulation and secrets scan\" --body-file /tmp/tpl/rust-issue.md"
